1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,500
It’s 2:00 A.M.

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,100
Your head is pounding,

3
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:06,600
and the only painkiller left in your cabinet expired three years ago.

4
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,200
You swallow it anyway.

5
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:11,500
Within 60 seconds, that pill hits your stomach acid.

6
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:13,400
And for the next 60 minutes,

7
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:17,200
a silent chemical chain reaction unfolds inside your organs.

8
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,800
For 90% of the pills in your house,

9
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:23,500
that expiration date on the bottle is a multi-billion-dollar myth.

10
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:25,200
But for the other 10%?

11
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:28,400
Swallowing them after they expire can trigger kidney damage,

12
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:32,800
bacterial infection, or a fatal medical failure in under an hour.

13
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:34,500
The clock just started.

14
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,600
Let’s look inside your stomach at Minute 5.

15
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:41,200
Right now, that old pill is soaking in stomach acid.

16
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,600
A fresh tablet is built like a microscopic time-bomb.

17
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:47,100
It’s held together by an invisible shield

18
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:50,000
that controls how fast the medicine enters your blood.

19
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
But where did you keep that bottle?

20
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
If you kept it in a warm, steamy bathroom cabinet,

21
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,500
something dangerous has already happened.

22
00:00:57,500 --> 00:00:59,400
Every time you take a hot shower,

23
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:02,800
microscopic humidity seeps through the pill's outer shell.

24
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,400
By Minute 15, a heat-damaged tablet does one of two things.

25
00:01:06,400 --> 00:01:08,400
Either it hardens like cement

26
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,600
and passes right through your body without working at all,

27
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,200
or the protective coating cracks open.

28
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:16,900
Instead of releasing slowly over six hours,

29
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:20,300
it dumps the entire chemical dose into your stomach at once,

30
00:01:20,300 --> 00:01:22,100
burning your stomach lining.

31
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:25,800
And yet, as those chemicals enter your bloodstream at Minute 20,

32
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,200
we hit a massive government secret.

33
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:32,000
What happens if your expired pill was kept in a cool, dry drawer?

34
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,200
It works.

35
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:38,200
In fact, the U.S. Military once sat on a $1 billion stockpile of expired medicine.

36
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:39,900
They didn't want to throw it away.

37
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:45,000
So they asked the FDA to secretly test over 3,000 batches of expired drugs.

38
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:47,600
The results shocked the medical world.

39
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:51,200
90% of those medications were still completely safe and potent

40
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,200
up to 15 years past their expiration date.

41
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:55,000
Why?

42
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,800
Because the date stamped on your bottle isn't the day the pill turns toxic.

43
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:03,200
It’s just the final day the company legally guarantees 100% strength.

44
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:04,000
Wait.

45
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,300
Before you swallow that old pill in your drawer,

46
00:02:06,300 --> 00:02:08,300
remember one critical detail.

47
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:12,500
The military stored their medicine in climate-controlled vaults, not your house.

48
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:15,000
And if you just swallowed one of the four household medications on the medical red list,

49
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Minute 38 is where your organs start paying the price.

50
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:18,400
Minute 38.

51
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:21,200
Your kidneys begin filtering your blood.

52
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:24,200
Every five minutes, all the blood in your body rushes through

53
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:28,000
millions of delicate microscopic tubes inside your kidneys.

54
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,800
While most pills simply get weaker with age,

55
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:34,800
certain degraded antibiotics—like older tetracycline formulas—

56
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:36,500
do something terrifying.

57
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:41,200
Heat and moisture cause their molecules to chemically twist into a poison.

58
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:44,000
As your kidneys filter these degraded molecules,

59
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,600
the poison attacks the filtration tubes.

60
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:50,000
It triggers a rare condition called Fanconi syndrome.

61
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,800
Your kidneys literally forget how to hold onto water, glucose, and potassium,

62
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:58,200
flushing vital life support straight out of your body.

63
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:02,200
Have you ever used expired cough syrup or old eye drops?

64
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:06,200
By Minute 45, liquid medicine becomes a biological trap.

65
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:08,200
Dry pills contain zero water,

66
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:10,500
so bacteria cannot grow inside them.

67
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:11,600
Liquids do.

68
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:16,000
Drug companies pack liquid medicines with chemical preservatives to kill germs every time you open the cap.

69
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,200
When a liquid medicine expires,

70
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:20,600
those preservatives die first.

71
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:24,000
Within 45 minutes of putting expired drops into your eyes,

72
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,200
or swallowing old liquid medicine,

73
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:28,200
you aren't just taking weak medicine.

74
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:33,000
You can be injecting a living colony of bacteria straight into your bloodstream.

75
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,800
Minute 52. We enter the red zone.

76
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:39,600
For a specific group of medicines, losing even 15% of their strength

77
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:42,000
means the difference between life and death.

78
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,500
Think about an EpiPen for severe allergies.

79
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:46,200
Insulin for diabetes.

80
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:49,400
Or nitroglycerin tablets used to stop a heart attack.

81
00:03:49,400 --> 00:03:52,600
Nitroglycerin is so unstable that once you open the bottle,

82
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:55,200
it starts evaporating into the air within months.

83
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:58,400
During chest pain, taking an expired tablet at Minute 1

84
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:02,000
means that by Minute 52, the blood vessels around your heart fail to open.

85
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,800
Then, at Minute 60, the scariest reaction of all begins.

86
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:08,600
If you took expired antibiotics for an infection,

87
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:12,500
the drug hits peak concentration in your blood right at the one-hour mark.

88
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:15,800
Because the pill has lost strength, it kills the weakest bacteria,

89
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:17,800
tricking you into feeling slightly better.

90
00:04:17,800 --> 00:04:20,200
But it leaves the strongest bacteria alive.

91
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:22,600
You just gave the surviving germs inside your body a training session,

92
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:25,000
helping them mutate into drug-resistant superbugs that modern medicine can no longer stop.

93
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Before you ever swallow an old pill,

94
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,200
run this three-second survival check.

95
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:30,800
One: Smell the bottle.

96
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:33,400
If old aspirin smells like sharp vinegar,

97
00:04:33,400 --> 00:04:36,800
or a capsule looks cracked, sticky, or faded—

98
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:38,400
throw it out immediately.

99
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:40,200
It is chemically breaking down.

100
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:45,800
Two: Never gamble with liquids, eye drops, insulin, heart pills, or antibiotics.

101
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:49,200
Three: Move your medicine out of the bathroom cabinet tonight.

102
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:53,000
Steam ages a pill faster than years on a calendar.

103
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,600
If you think an expired pill does strange things to your organs,

104
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:01,600
wait until you see what happens to your brain when you go just 72 hours without sleep.

105
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:04,000
Click the video on your screen right now—

106
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
because by Hour 48, your brain starts eating itself.